    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green (the single acceptance test, `coreLogsAuditOnCreate`, now passes).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now emits an audit line on successful create.\n\n**New step class** \u2014 `AuditOwnerCreated.java`:\n```java\nprivate static final Logger AUDIT = LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"AUDIT\");\n\npublic void service(@Val Owner owner) {\n    AUDIT.info(\"Owner created id={} customerCode={} registrationDate={}\",\n            owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), owner.getRegistrationDate());\n}\n```\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 inserted an `audit` step in `owners.POST.yml` between `save` and `respond` (under the same `transaction` governance):\n```yaml\nsave:\n  ...\n  next: audit\naudit:\n  class: ...owner.AuditOwnerCreated\n  govern: [ transaction ]\n  next: respond\n```\n\n## Design notes\n\n- **Single responsibility**: auditing is its own small function class rather than bolted onto `SaveOwner` or `RespondWithOwnerCreated`, matching the project's one-step-per-concern OfficeFloor convention.\n- **Placement matters**: it runs *after* `SaveOwner`, so the persisted owner id is populated. It reads the already-built owner via `@Val` (no new body binding, no repository call), so it carries the same `customerCode` and `registrationDate` the response and later reads return \u2014 which is exactly what the test asserts against the fetched owner (`id`, `customerCode`, `registrationDate` as an ISO `yyyy-MM-dd` string, e.g. `AUDIT - Owner created id=11 customerCode=TOW-SUR-0001 registrationDate=2026-09-18`).\n- **Logger name**: uses the dedicated `AUDIT` logger the test's `AuditLogCapture` attaches to, following the existing `LoggerFactory` idiom used in `GeneralExceptionHandler`.",
